//! The Window module: window creation over SDL2's C API — Jai's `Window_Creation`.
//!
//! Narrowed to that in Group D of the Simp-shaped-graphics restructure (ADR-0182). It used to be a window
//! *and* a renderer *and* an event queue; the renderer is `modules/Simp` now and the events are
//! `modules/Input`. Jai splits the three, and the split is load-bearing rather than tidy: **all the
//! per-platform work lives in window creation**, which is what lets one portable rendering API sit on top.
//!
//! # Why SDL2 and not Cocoa
//!
//! PLAN §2.1 imagined "Cocoa via `#foreign`", and every Cocoa call goes through `objc_msgSend`, which is
//! **variadic** — and a variadic call is E0289 here because Cranelift's `Signature` has no notion of a
//! variadic boundary (ADR-0162 §2). That blocker is upstream, so it is not a matter of doing more work in
//! this project.
//!
//! SDL2 needs none of it: every routine below is a plain C function taking scalars and pointers. It also
//! works on Linux, which §0's decision #6 makes a target and which Cocoa never was — and on Windows, which
//! matters here because `-lSDL2` is the link name on all three while OpenGL has three different ones
//! (ADR-0182 §2). The cost is that a drawing program needs SDL2 installed and that this module binds
//! somebody else's library rather than the platform — stated rather than glossed, and argued at length in
//! ADR-0163 §1.
//!
//! # Using it
//!
//! SDL2 is not in the C driver's default search path, so a build needs to be told where it is:
//!
//! ```text
//! jr build my_program.jr -L /opt/homebrew/lib
//! ```
//!
//! or `JR_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/homebrew/lib`. That is a property of the machine compiling and deliberately not
//! something a source file can say (ADR-0163 §2).
//!
//! **This module does not run under `jr run`.** The comptime VM resolves foreign symbols from the compiler's
//! own process image rather than from a link line (ADR-0158 §3's mechanism), so it can reach libc and nothing
//! else. A program that draws is a `jr build` program.
//!
//! # Why the handle is `*u8` and not an opaque type
//!
//! `SDL_Window` is opaque in C — a caller only ever holds a pointer. A Jairs `Window :: struct { handle:
//! *u8; }` adds a name and one field, and the name is worth having, so that is what the wrapper below is.
//! The *raw* bindings take `*u8` because that is what an opaque pointer is, and because a `#foreign`
//! signature naming a Jairs struct it never dereferences would be describing a layout SDL2 does not have.
//!
//! # What is absent, and why
//!
//! **No drawing.** `modules/Simp` owns the renderer, and a second way to draw would be the opposite of a
//! clean cutover — which is why `set_color`, `clear`, `fill`, `outline`, `line` and `present` were deleted
//! rather than deprecated.
//!
//! **No events.** `modules/Input` owns the queue and the `SDL_Event` overlay.
//!
//! **No texture or image loading.** `modules/Image` owns that.

/// `SDL_INIT_VIDEO`, the only subsystem this module needs.
///
/// 0x20. The flags are a bitmask and stable across SDL2's lifetime, unlike the `O_*` constants `File` had to
/// hedge about (ADR-0157 §1): SDL defines them itself rather than inheriting a platform's.
INIT_VIDEO :: 32;

/// `SDL_WINDOWPOS_CENTERED`, for a window with no preferred corner.
///
/// 0x2FFF0000. A magic number in the header too — SDL computes it as `SDL_WINDOWPOS_CENTERED_MASK | 0`, and
/// the mask is what this is.
CENTERED :: 805240832;

/// `SDL_WINDOW_SHOWN` — the window is visible immediately.
SHOWN :: 4;

/// `SDL_WINDOW_HIDDEN` — created but not mapped.
///
/// What a test wants: it exercises the whole creation path without requiring a display, which is why the
/// probe behind ADR-0163 §1 used it.
HIDDEN :: 8;

/// `SDL_WINDOW_RESIZABLE`.
RESIZABLE :: 32;

/// `SDL_RENDERER_ACCELERATED` — prefer a GPU-backed renderer.
ACCELERATED :: 2;

/// `SDL_RENDERER_SOFTWARE` — force the software path, which any machine has.
SOFTWARE :: 1;

/// `SDL_RENDERER_PRESENTVSYNC`.
PRESENT_VSYNC :: 4;

/// An `SDL_Rect`: four C `int`s.
///
/// `s32` fields, matching C's `int` on both supported targets, and **not** `s64`: SDL reads sixteen bytes
/// through the pointer, so a wider field would make the second rectangle's worth of data land in the first's
/// height. Passed by pointer, as SDL's own API does, so ADR-0161's aggregate-by-value work is not needed here
/// — which is part of why SDL2 was reachable before Cocoa was.
Rect :: struct {
    /// Left edge, in pixels from the drawing area's left.
    x: s32;
    /// Top edge, in pixels from the top.
    y: s32;
    /// Width in pixels.
    w: s32;
    /// Height in pixels.
    h: s32;
}

/// Whether `Rect` is the 16 bytes SDL2 reads through the pointer.
///
/// A file-scope constant, which ADR-0180 §3 made possible. `Rect` is the one layout claim this module still
/// makes — the `Event` overlay's moved to `Input` and became `EVENT_LAYOUT_IS_SDL2` there — and it is worth
/// checking for the reason its own docs give: four `s32`s and four `s64`s are the same *shape* and different
/// bytes, so a widened field puts the second rectangle's data in the first's height.
RECT_LAYOUT_IS_SDL2 :: size_of(Rect) == 16;

/// A window.
///
/// A named one-field struct rather than a bare pointer, for the reason `File` wraps a descriptor (ADR-0157
/// §1): a handle and a count are both machine words, and the compiler will not confuse them once one has a
/// name.
Window :: struct {
    /// The `SDL_Window *`. Null when the window is not open.
    handle: *u8;
}

/// Starts the video subsystem.
///
/// `#must`: nothing else here works if this failed, and a caller who ignores the flag gets a null window and
/// a null renderer and no idea why — the exact chain of one-level-removed failures the marker exists for
/// (ADR-0151).
///
/// Idempotent in SDL, so calling it twice is defined and cheap.
start :: () -> bool #must {
    return sdl_init(cast(u32, INIT_VIDEO)) == 0;
}

/// Shuts the video subsystem down. Not `#must`: there is nothing to recover from and nothing to report.
stop :: () {
    sdl_quit();
}

/// Opens a window `width` by `height`, centred, with `flags`.
///
/// **Jai's `create_window`**, and the name is a correction rather than a rename: this was `open`, which
/// collided with `File.open` — so a program that both drew and read a file could not name either
/// (`error[E0211]: ambiguous name `open``). ADR-0179 makes a qualified import the general answer; the name
/// changes anyway, because `create_window` says what it does and because an exported name is a claim on
/// every importer that spells it bare.
///
/// `title` must be NUL-terminated, which is why it is a `*u8` and not a `string`: SDL reads to a NUL and a
/// Jairs `string` carries a count instead. `File.nul_terminated`'s trick — a scratch copy from temporary
/// storage — is the way to pass a computed title, and it is deliberately not done here: a window title
/// usually outlives the call, and temporary storage does not.
///
/// `#must`, and the flag is the honest answer rather than a null check: SDL fails to create a window for
/// reasons a caller can act on — no display, an unsupported flag combination — and a null handle read as
/// "not yet" instead of "never" is how a draw loop spins forever.
create_window :: (title: *u8, width: s64, height: s64, flags: s64) -> (Window, bool) #must {
    w: Window;
    // Every constant is cast at the boundary, which is the discipline the private section's own
    // comment argues for: a Jairs constant is `s64` and there is no syntax to annotate one narrower
    // (probed — `CENTERED : s32 : 805240832` does not parse), so the narrowing is explicit here.
    w.handle = sdl_create_window(title, cast(s32, CENTERED), cast(s32, CENTERED), cast(s32, width), cast(s32, height), cast(u32, flags));
    return w, w.handle != null;
}

/// Closes `window` and marks it closed.
///
/// Safe on an already-closed window, so a caller can close on every path without tracking whether they got
/// one — the property that makes `File.close` usable where a `defer` would go (ADR-0157 §3).
///
/// **Destroy the renderer first.** SDL destroys a window's renderer with it, so closing in the other order
/// leaves `Simp` holding a dangling handle. Stated because nothing enforces it: the renderer lives in
/// another module and neither struct points at the other.
close :: (window: *Window) {
    if window.handle != null {
        sdl_destroy_window(window.handle);
    }
    window.handle = null;
}

/// The window's size in pixels, as the window manager has it.
///
/// **Not the render target's size**, which is `Simp.get_render_dimensions` — on a high-DPI display the two
/// genuinely differ, and a program that sized its geometry from this one would draw at half scale. Both
/// exist because both questions get asked: this one is about the window, that one about the pixels.
///
/// `(0, 0)` for a closed window, which is the same "nothing there" answer every other routine here gives.
get_window_size :: (window: *Window) -> (s64, s64) {
    if window.handle == null {
        return 0, 0;
    }
    w: s32;
    h: s32;
    sdl_get_window_size(window.handle, *w, *h);
    return cast(s64, w), cast(s64, h);
}

/// Sleeps for `milliseconds`.
///
/// Here rather than in `Time` because `Time` deliberately has no sleep (ADR-0155 §1: a blocking call in the
/// comptime VM would mean compilation that pauses) — and this module already cannot run in the VM at all, so
/// the objection does not apply. A drawing program needs to yield the CPU between frames, and this is the
/// only way it can.
delay :: (milliseconds: s64) {
    sdl_delay(cast(u32, milliseconds));
}

/// A rectangle, built from four `s64`s a caller already has.
///
/// A constructor because the four `cast(s32, …)`es are noise at every call site, and because narrowing is
/// exactly where a caller would forget one and write a `s64` into a field SDL reads as two.
rect :: (x: s64, y: s64, w: s64, h: s64) -> Rect {
    r: Rect;
    r.x = cast(s32, x);
    r.y = cast(s32, y);
    r.w = cast(s32, w);
    r.h = cast(s32, h);
    return r;
}

/// How many video drivers SDL was built with.
///
/// Not useful for drawing, and useful for a *test*: it is the cheapest call that proves the library linked
/// and ran, with no display and no window. It is what ADR-0163 §1's first probe used.
video_driver_count :: () -> s64 {
    return cast(s64, sdl_video_drivers());
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Private: the raw SDL2 bindings
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Everything below is **not exported** (ADR-0054 §2, ADR-0179 §6). These are the raw C symbols and the
// library handle; a caller wants `create_window`, not `sdl_create_window`.
//
// **The widths are C's** (ADR-0182 §2). Every binding here used to take and return `s64` even where SDL
// declares `int`, `Uint32` or `Uint8` — which contradicted this file's own `Rect`, whose docs explain at
// length why a narrow field matters at a C boundary. Correcting them is unambiguously right on every
// target, and it is a prerequisite for trusting the boundary on Windows x64, whose first four arguments are
// register-passed and whose remainder are stack-passed. The *wrapper* parameters above stay `s64`, so a
// caller needs no casts: the narrowing happens once, here, exactly as `rect` already did it.
#scope_module

/// The SDL2 shared library.
///
/// Named `"SDL2"`, which becomes `-lSDL2` — the same name on macOS, Linux and Windows, which is the whole
/// reason this library and not OpenGL. Where it lives is the operator's business; see the module docs.
sdl :: #system_library "SDL2";

/// `SDL_Init(Uint32 flags)`.
sdl_init :: (flags: u32) -> s32 #foreign sdl "SDL_Init";

/// `SDL_Quit`.
sdl_quit :: () #foreign sdl "SDL_Quit";

/// `SDL_CreateWindow(const char *title, int x, int y, int w, int h, Uint32 flags)`.
sdl_create_window :: (title: *u8, x: s32, y: s32, w: s32, h: s32, flags: u32) -> *u8 #foreign sdl "SDL_CreateWindow";

/// `SDL_DestroyWindow`.
sdl_destroy_window :: (window: *u8) #foreign sdl "SDL_DestroyWindow";

/// `SDL_GetWindowSize(SDL_Window *, int *w, int *h)` — returns nothing, writes through both pointers.
sdl_get_window_size :: (window: *u8, w: *s32, h: *s32) #foreign sdl "SDL_GetWindowSize";

/// `SDL_GetNumVideoDrivers`.
sdl_video_drivers :: () -> s32 #foreign sdl "SDL_GetNumVideoDrivers";

/// `SDL_Delay(Uint32 ms)`.
sdl_delay :: (milliseconds: u32) #foreign sdl "SDL_Delay";
